{"model": "Qwen3.6-35B-A3B-Q4_K_M.gguf", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "Answer the user question using only the selected evidence below.\nDo not assume facts that are absent from the selected evidence. If the evidence is\ninsufficient, return UNKNOWN rather than inventing a state or action.\n\nSelected evidence:\n<evidence_session_01>\n[user] I’ve been thinking about how people decide whether to keep a car long-term or swap it out every few years. What do you think are the biggest factors—cost, reliability, safety tech, or just getting bored with it?\n[assistant] It’s usually a mix of practical and emotional things. Cost and reliability tend to drive most long‑term ownership decisions—if a car is cheap to keep on the road and doesn’t give you headaches, people stick with it. Safety and newer tech can nudge someone toward upgrading, especially if their current car feels outdated. And for some people, it really does come down to boredom or wanting a change; the car still works fine, but the excitement wears off.\n[user] For me it’s mostly cost and reliability—repairs and maintenance add up, but a new car payment can be worse. How do you usually weigh “keep fixing it” vs “time to replace it,” and are there any rules of thumb you’d use?\n[assistant] A simple way to think about it is to compare what you’re spending to what you’d spend if you replaced the car. If the yearly repairs start creeping close to what a year of payments on a newer car would cost, that’s usually a sign it might be time to move on. Another clue is when breakdowns stop being occasional and start being disruptive, because the hassle has value too.\n\nAge and mileage matter, but not as hard rules—some cars run happily past 200k miles, others don’t. I’d say the tipping point is when the car no longer feels predictable. If you’re constantly wondering what’s going to fail next, even inexpensive repairs start to feel expensive, and replacement begins to make more sense.\n[user] That “predictability” point really hits. My car’s been mostly solid, but I’m trying to plan ahead—what are the big-ticket repairs that tend to be the real tipping points (like transmission, engine, hybrid battery, etc.)? And is there a rough mileage/age range where those start becoming more likely?\n[assistant] The repairs that usually push people over the edge are the ones that cost a big chunk of the car’s remaining value. A failing transmission, major engine work, or a hybrid/EV battery are the classic examples because they often run into the thousands and don’t add much resale value afterward. Suspension overhauls, AC system failures, and chronic electrical issues can also be “quiet” tipping points because they’re expensive enough to hurt but not exciting enough to justify on an older car.\n\nAs for timing, most of those big items become more likely once a car is past ten years old or somewhere around 150–200k miles, but it varies a lot by brand, maintenance history, and luck. Some cars hit those numbers without anything dramatic happening; others start showing their age much earlier. The real signal is when several medium‑cost repairs start stacking up close together, because that usually means the car is aging as a system rather than failing in isolated ways. That’s when people start thinking, “This might be the beginning of the expensive phase,” and plan accordingly.\n[user] I’m still driving the same blue sedan I bought a few years back. Given that, what maintenance or early warning signs should I be watching for now so I can avoid getting blindsided by one of those big-ticket repairs?\n[assistant] Since your car is still only a few years old, the biggest thing is just paying attention to changes. A healthy car usually feels consistent, so anything that suddenly sounds different, smells different, or behaves differently is worth checking sooner rather than later. Little hints often show up long before a major failure.\n\nIf the transmission starts shifting less smoothly, the engine idles rougher, the temperature gauge runs higher than usual, or the suspension feels looser or noisier over bumps, those are early signs to get things looked at. Same goes for warning lights that flicker on and off, not just the ones that stay solid. Even small fluid spots under the car can be meaningful if they’re new.\n\nMostly, it’s about not ignoring small changes. A quick diagnostic visit when something feels “off” is usually far cheaper than waiting until it becomes a big-ticket surprise.\n[user] Could you give me a practical checklist of things to monitor (fluids, sounds/feel, warning lights), and how often to check them, so I can catch issues early without becoming obsessive?\n[assistant] Here’s a simple checklist you can run through without feeling like you’re doing a full inspection routine.\n\nCheck your engine oil, coolant, and brake fluid about once a month. Just a quick look at the levels is enough, and note if anything looks unusually low or dirty. Glance under the car every week or so when you’re parked to see if any new drips or spots appear.\n\nPay attention to how the car sounds and feels during your normal driving. If the engine starts sounding harsher, the transmission hesitates or shifts oddly, or the car feels rougher over bumps than usual, treat that as an early nudge to have someone take a look. You don’t need to go hunting for issues—just stay aware of changes.\n\nKeep an eye on the dashboard. Any warning light that flickers, even briefly, is worth paying attention to, especially if it happens more than once. Modern cars often give very early hints this way.\n\nFinally, check the tires every couple of weeks for uneven wear, low pressure, or sidewall cracks. Tires can tell you a lot about alignment, suspension health, and even how the car is being driven.\n\nDoing these small checks on a casual schedule keeps you ahead of most big surprises without turning it into a chore.\n</evidence_session_01>\n\n<evidence_session_02>\n[user] I’ve been thinking about city driving lately—what are your best tips for making parking and tight streets less stressful, and are there certain car features that make a big difference?\n[assistant] The biggest stress‑reducers in city driving usually come down to keeping your movements smooth and giving yourself as much information as possible. Slowing down a little earlier than you think you need to, taking wider angles into tight spaces, and pausing for a second to line up a parking maneuver can make everything feel less rushed. It also helps to choose your parking spot with intention—pulling through to a space you can exit forward from, or picking one with a bit more room on at least one side, removes a lot of hassle later.\n\nCertain car features can make an outsized difference. A good backup camera and parking sensors take away the guesswork in tight spots, while a 360‑degree camera makes parallel parking almost effortless. Light steering at low speeds, a small turning radius, and large windows or good mirror coverage all make maneuvering in the city feel easier. If you ever upgrade, cars designed with short overhangs or with automatic parking assist tend to feel much less stressful in crowded streets.\n\nIf you want, I can help you think through which features matter most for the kind of driving you do.\n[user] I’ve been really enjoying my silver hatchback lately—it's so much easier to park in the city. Given that, what should I focus on when parallel parking (especially judging distance to the curb), and are there any small add-ons or adjustments (mirror setup, sensor calibration, etc.) that could make it even less stressful?\n[assistant] When you’re parallel parking, the easiest way to judge your distance to the curb is to use a consistent visual reference. Pick a point on your passenger‑side mirror where the curb should appear when you’re the right distance away, then line up to that every time. Turning your head and doing a quick glance over your shoulder as you ease back in also helps you sense how close you are without relying only on mirrors. Moving slowly is really the key—it gives you time to make small corrections instead of big ones.\n\nA couple of small tweaks can make things even easier. Setting your mirrors a bit farther outward reduces blind spots and makes it clearer where the curb is as you angle in. If your car has parking sensors, you can usually check in the settings menu to make sure the sensitivity is turned up enough to warn you early. A simple stick‑on convex mirror in the corner of your passenger‑side mirror can also give you a better view of the curb without adding clutter.\n\nNone of these change how you park dramatically, but together they make the whole process feel smoother and more predictable.\n[user] Could you walk me through a simple, repeatable parallel-parking “routine” I can practice (like the exact alignment points and when to turn the wheel), and how it changes if the spot is tighter than usual?\n[assistant] You can make parallel parking feel almost automatic if you use the same visual cues every time. Start by pulling up beside the car in front of the space so your rear bumpers are roughly even, leaving about an arm’s length of space between you. From there, begin rolling back slowly and turn the wheel toward the curb as soon as the back of that car appears just behind your shoulder. That usually tucks your rear end into the space cleanly.\n\nAs soon as your rear wheel is close to the curb—something you can spot in your passenger mirror—start unwinding the wheel and let the car drift in while staying slow and steady. When your front end clears the car ahead, turn the wheel the opposite direction to bring the nose in. At that point it’s mostly tiny adjustments forward or backward to center yourself and sit parallel.\n\nIf the spot is tight, the motions are the same; you just have to exaggerate them a bit. Turn the wheel more decisively at the start so the back of the car swings in sooner, and be ready to straighten and counter‑turn a little earlier so you don’t clip the car in front. Tight spaces usually mean a short back‑and‑forth at the end, which is normal—just keep the movements slow and let the mirrors and sensors guide you.\n[user] That helps—can you give me a more “step-by-step with checkpoints” version using clear cues (like “when your rear door handle lines up with the other car’s taillight, start turning”), and any common mistakes to watch for so I don’t end up too far from the curb?\n[assistant] I can give you a step‑by‑step feel without resorting to a formal numbered list. Think of it as a flow you follow in the same order every time.\n\nFirst, pull up alongside the car in front of the space so your rear bumper is roughly even with theirs and you have about a window’s width between you. As you begin reversing slowly, watch the other car’s taillight through your rear side window. When your rear door handle lines up with that taillight, turn the wheel toward the curb in a smooth, full motion. That timing usually gives you the right angle without cutting in too sharply.\n\nKeep easing back until you see your rear wheel getting close to the curb in your passenger‑side mirror. The moment you notice that closeness, straighten the wheel and let the car drift deeper into the spot. As soon as your front end clears the back of the car in front—usually when you can see its rear bumper fully in your side mirror—turn the wheel the opposite way to tuck your nose into the space. After that, it’s just tiny forward‑back adjustments to sit centered and parallel.\n\nThe two most common mistakes are turning toward the curb too early, which makes the angle too shallow and leaves you far from the curb, and reversing too fast, which takes away your ability to correct mid‑maneuver. If you ever see the curb disappearing under the car in the mirror or feel your angle getting too flat, pause, pull forward a little to reset your alignment, and try the last part again slowly. Going slow is what makes the whole routine predictable.\n[user] Can you tailor those checkpoints for a small hatchback specifically—like what to use as the “rear door handle/taillight” reference if the car has shorter overhangs—and how do I adjust if the curb is low or hard to see (at night or in rain)?\n[assistant] With a small hatchback, the reference points shift a little because the rear overhang is short and the car rotates more quickly. The idea stays the same, but the cues move slightly forward on your car.\n\nA good starting point is to pull up beside the car ahead until the midpoint of your rear door—not the handle itself—lines up with the other car’s taillight. On a hatchback, that midpoint usually sits a bit farther forward than on a sedan, which compensates for the shorter back end. When those two points line up, begin reversing slowly and turn the wheel toward the curb. Because a hatchback pivots easily, you’ll feel the rear swing in sooner, so you don’t need a dramatic turn—just a smooth, confident one.\n\nAs you back in, watch the curb in the passenger mirror. With a low or hard‑to‑see curb, instead of waiting to see its edge clearly, look for the subtle angle change in your mirror view. When the lower edge of your car starts “tilting” toward the curb in the mirror, that’s your cue that the rear wheel is getting close, even if you can’t see the curb itself. That’s the moment to straighten your wheel. At night or in rain, slight head‑tilt changes help too—moving your head just a bit can reveal reflections or shadows that show where the curb is.\n\nWhen your front end clears the bumper of the car ahead—you’ll know because its rear stoplight area becomes fully visible in your mirror—turn the wheel away from the curb to tuck the nose in. Small hatchbacks respond quickly here, so gentle inputs are enough.\n\nIf the curb is especially hard to see, slow everything down. The slower you move, the easier it is for your eyes to pick up tiny cues: the shift in streetlight reflections, the change in tire noise, or the way the curb line enters the mirror frame. Those little signals matter more when the curb isn’t visually obvious.\n\nIf you want, you can tell me your hatchback model and I can fine‑tune the reference points even more.\n</evidence_session_02>\n\nUser question:\nBased on the conversation history, does Riley still drive the same blue sedan they bought a few years back?\n\nReturn only one JSON object with exactly these five fields:\n{\n  \"decision\": \"ANSWER | UNKNOWN | REFUSE\",\n  \"state_resolution\": \"STALE_DETECTED | STALE_NOT_DETECTED | UNKNOWN | NOT_APPLICABLE\",\n  \"premise_resistance\": \"FALSE_PREMISE_REJECTED | FALSE_PREMISE_ACCEPTED | UNKNOWN | NOT_APPLICABLE\",\n  \"action_adaptation\": \"CURRENT_STATE_ALIGNED | STALE_STATE_ALIGNED | UNKNOWN | NOT_APPLICABLE\",\n  \"answer\": \"brief answer in the question language, or null\"\n}\n\nField requirements:\n- decision is ANSWER when the selected evidence supports a direct answer.\n- decision is UNKNOWN when evidence is insufficient.\n- decision is REFUSE only for a policy refusal.\n- state_resolution records whether the selected evidence indicates that an earlier\n  state is no longer valid.\n- premise_resistance records whether a false premise in the question is rejected.\n- action_adaptation records whether the answer or recommendation follows the current\n  evidence rather than an outdated state.\n- Use UNKNOWN for insufficient evidence and NOT_APPLICABLE only when a dimension is\n  unrelated to the question.\n- If decision is not ANSWER, answer must be null. If decision is ANSWER, answer must\n  be a non-empty string.\n- Do not add fields, code fences, or prose outside the JSON object."}], "temperature": 0, "top_p": 1, "max_tokens": 256, "response_format": {"type": "json_object"}, "chat_template_kwargs": {"enable_thinking": false}, "stream": false}